# Function A
def reverse_string(s):
    return s[::-1]
    # O(n), Because the memory scales to the size of the data input. This is because it takes the string and returns a new
    string in reverse order.

# Function B
def count_letters(text):
    counts = {}
    for char in text:
        counts[char] = counts.get(char, 0) + 1
    return counts
    O(n). Memory scales to the size of the data input. This takes a string and turns it into a new dict. The dict only
    gets unique char as count loops through the data

# Function C
def matrix_identity(n):
    return [[1 if i == j else 0 for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]
    O(n^2) It returns a grid that is where we get the n^2. If you pass 3 in for n then you get a 3 rows
    with 3 values per row total is 9.

# Function D
def running_sum(numbers):
    total = 0
    for num in numbers:
        total += num
    print(total)
    O(1) The total grows as the loop runs but the data inside total only increases it will always be one number. print only gives you the value
    of the variable.